INT. SLEEPING CAR, NIGHT TRAIN - NIGHT

Brass lamps swing with the rails. Rain beads the window. On the table between the seats, a chess set mid-game and an untouched bottle of wine.

BARNABAS HOLT (50s, silver beard) leans back with his hands folded. Opposite him sits LEOPOLD VANCE (50s, silver moustache), staring at the board. At the door, the conductor, ISADORA FEYERABEND (30s, brass buttons), has just finished punching tickets.

BARNABAS
Your move.

LEOPOLD
I know.

ISADORA
Gentlemen, we reach Vienna at dawn.

LEOPOLD
Plenty of time to lose.

BARNABAS
Time for you to lose.

ISADORA
You've both been playing since Prague.

LEOPOLD
He cheats.

BARNABAS
He sulks.

ISADORA
One of you left this on the floor.

She holds up a small brass key. Barnabas reaches for it. Leopold reaches too. They both stop, hands in the air. He looks at the key. He looks at the other man. He knows.

LEOPOLD
That opens the trunk in the baggage car.

BARNABAS
Does it?

ISADORA
It does. And the trunk is empty.

She closes the door behind her. The train rattles on through the dark.
